From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: 1ma <hello@1ma.ai>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 22:46:13 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] 1ma: built-in 1ma plugins take over verified installed copies

Before patch 0014 the 1ma plugins were installed from 1ma's repository (git)
or from the Linux release folder (path). When a built-in 1ma plugin meets a
copy with its id, reconcileBundled now moves that row to the built-in source
instead of skipping it, keeping the enablement and, through the plugin id,
the settings, secrets, schedules and data. Only a verifiable 1ma source
qualifies: git from github.com/1ma-ai/1ma (compared by host and path) with
subdirectory plugins/<name>, or a folder carrying the release script's
.1ma-release.json; a copy newer than the built-in is kept. Every other copy
stays and the log says why.

Before a copy is taken over or a 1ma plugin is first built in, automatically
or by hand (builtin:<name>), a copy of the whole database from before the
first move (<data>/backups/before-1ma-builtins.db, a last resort, never
replaced) and a per-plugin record of how each was installed (the git tag, or
the release commit from .1ma-release.json) exist. If the copy fails, copies
stay and an install waits or is refused.

The way back: an app from before 0014 deletes, with their settings, secrets
and schedules, the rows whose source is a built-in it does not know, while bb
deletes a plugin's data only on removal and installing a plugin keeps the
data under its id. server/dist/one-ma-rollback.js, run with 1ma stopped,
holds bb-app's runtime claim on the data folder (refusing while 1ma runs)
and, in one exclusive transaction, moves the built-in 1ma rows into a pending
rollback (app_settings_values "1ma.rollback") and removes them; it says how
each was installed before and whether to turn it off again or leave it out.
While a rollback is pending, every built-in 1ma row, automatic or installed
by hand, is written by an immediate transaction that first finds none
pending, so no start of this version can hand the older one a row to delete.
--resume ends it all at once or not at all: a removed row comes back exactly
as it was, unless the older version installed that plugin again from a
source 1ma can take over (taken over at the next start, or left installed
when newer than the built-in, by the takeover's own rule). A copy from any
other source is a conflict: nothing changes and the command fails, until
--replace-other-copies unregisters such copies keeping the data under their
ids (a plain removal would delete it) and brings the built-ins back.
